1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
One sky. Those looking for south saw —
three crosses.

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:10,000
One true, one false, one diamond. Mix
them up and you lose south.

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:16,000
The smallest constellation: four bright
stars in a kite, plus a faint fifth.

4
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:23,000
Extend the long bar about 4.5 times: you
land within 3° of the south celestial
pole.

5
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:38,000
About 30° west. No bright pointers
beside it, no Coalsack.

6
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:44,000
Try the same trick: you miss the pole by
more than 20°.

7
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:59,000
A name from modern stargazers: closer to
the pole, all four stars in Carina.

8
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:06,000
From 22° N the cross stands upright,
5–11° up. North of about 33° N, it never
rises.

9
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:13,000
Before the compass: finding the way by
stars. Follow for a new sky every week.

